      teachingContent: `### Arrangement Reveals Meaning

The twelve loaves of showbread were not heaped carelessly upon the table. They were arranged in **two precise rows of six** (Leviticus 24:6), with pure frankincense placed upon each row. The arrangement was deliberate, purposeful, and rich with meaning — the twelve loaves representing the twelve tribes, the two rows perhaps reflecting the two tablets of the law or the dual nature of God's covenant with His people. The arrangement itself was part of the message. In the same way, every passage of Scripture has an **arrangement** — a historical setting, a cultural backdrop, a literary structure — and understanding this arrangement is essential to grasping the passage's true meaning.

### Historical and Cultural Context

When Ezra and the Levites read the law to the returned exiles, they did not merely recite words. Nehemiah 8:8 records that they "read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading." **They explained the context.** They helped the people understand what the words meant within their historical and cultural setting. The Ethiopian eunuch in Acts 8 was reading Isaiah 53 but could not understand it without help: "How can I, except some man should guide me?" (Acts 8:31). Philip then explained the passage by placing it in its proper context — the prophecy of Christ's suffering. Context is the bridge between the ancient text and the modern reader. Without it, we build castles on sand.

### Three Layers of Context

Faithful Bible study attends to **three layers of context**. First, the **historical context**: Who wrote this? When? To whom? Under what circumstances? What was happening in the world at that time? Second, the **literary context**: What comes before and after this passage? How does it fit within the chapter, the book, and the section of the Bible in which it appears? What is the genre — narrative, poetry, prophecy, law, epistle, or apocalyptic? Third, the **theological context**: How does this passage connect to the grand narrative of Scripture — creation, fall, redemption, and restoration? Paul wrote, "Now all these things happened unto them for ensamples: and they are written for our admonition, upon whom the ends of the world are come" (1 Corinthians 10:11). The events of Scripture were real history written for our instruction, and understanding their context unlocks their application to our lives.

### The Danger of Proof-Texting

One of the most common errors in Bible study is **proof-texting** — pulling a single verse out of its context and using it to support a predetermined conclusion. Peter warned that "no prophecy of the scripture is of any private interpretation" (2 Peter 1:20). Scripture was not given for us to twist to our own purposes but to understand as God intended. The showbread was arranged according to divine instruction, not human preference. Similarly, we must handle the text according to its divinely ordered context, not rearrange it to fit our agendas. The student who masters context will avoid a multitude of interpretive errors and will discover depths of meaning that the surface reader never sees.`,
