      teachingContent: `### The Covenant Bread

The showbread was also called **"the bread of the covenant"** because it symbolized the perpetual covenant relationship between God and Israel. Every Sabbath the loaves were renewed, reminding the nation that God's covenant faithfulness is unending. The Old Testament is the covenant foundation upon which the entire Bible rests. Without Genesis, we do not understand the origin of sin or the promise of a Redeemer. Without Exodus, we do not grasp the meaning of the Passover lamb. Without Leviticus, the sanctuary truths that undergird our faith remain sealed. Without the Psalms, we lack the vocabulary of worship. Without the prophets, we have no prophetic framework for understanding history. Paul declared, "For whatsoever things were written aforetime were written for our learning, that we through patience and comfort of the scriptures might have hope" (Romans 15:4). The Old Testament is not obsolete; it is **foundational**.

### Studying Old Testament Narrative

The Old Testament contains vast stretches of **narrative** — the stories of Abraham, Joseph, Moses, David, Elijah, Daniel, and dozens more. Studying narrative requires specific skills. We must pay attention to character development, plot structure, dialogue, and the narrator's commentary. We must ask: What is God doing in this story? What does the character's response reveal about faith or failure? How does this narrative fit into the larger story of redemption? The story of Josiah discovering the lost book of the law in the temple (2 Chronicles 34:14-21) is particularly instructive: when the king heard the words of the law, he tore his robes in repentance. **The Word had been lost and forgotten**, and its rediscovery brought national revival. How many believers today have a Bible on their shelf but have lost the Word in their hearts?

### Studying Old Testament Law and Poetry

The **law** (Torah) is not merely a list of ancient rules but a revelation of God's holy character and His design for human flourishing. The ceremonial laws point to Christ's sacrifice and priestly ministry; the moral law (the Ten Commandments) is an eternal transcript of God's character; the civil laws reveal principles of justice and mercy that still speak today. **Old Testament poetry** — the Psalms, Proverbs, Job, Ecclesiastes, Song of Solomon — uses parallelism, imagery, and emotional intensity to communicate truth that doctrinal propositions alone cannot capture. The blessed man of Psalm 1 "delights in the law of the LORD" and "meditates therein day and night" (Psalm 1:2), and the result is a life of fruitfulness and stability. Learning to read poetry as poetry, law as law, and narrative as narrative is essential to faithful Old Testament study.

### Christ in the Old Testament

After His resurrection, Jesus walked with two disciples on the road to Emmaus and performed the greatest Bible study in history: **"Beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded unto them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself"** (Luke 24:27). Christ is hidden in every page of the Old Testament — in the sacrificial system, in the feasts, in the prophecies, in the types and shadows. The student who learns to find Christ in the Old Testament discovers that the entire Bible is a unified testimony to the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. The bread of the covenant is, ultimately, the Bread of Life Himself.`,
