      teachingContent: `### From Shadow to Substance

In John chapter 6, Jesus stood before a crowd that had just been fed by the miraculous multiplication of loaves and declared, **"I am the bread of life: he that cometh to me shall never hunger; and he that believeth on me shall never thirst"** (John 6:35). The showbread in the sanctuary had always pointed to this moment — the moment when the shadow gave way to the substance, when the type met its Antitype in the person of Jesus Christ. The New Testament is the fulfillment of everything the Old Testament promised. "God, who at sundry times and in divers manners spake in time past unto the fathers by the prophets, hath in these last days spoken unto us by his Son" (Hebrews 1:1-2). To study the New Testament is to feast on the bread of fulfillment, to see how every prophecy, every type, every promise finds its "yes and amen" in Christ.

### Studying the Gospels

The four Gospels — Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John — present the life, teachings, death, and resurrection of Jesus from four complementary perspectives. **Matthew** writes to a Jewish audience, presenting Jesus as the Messiah-King who fulfills Old Testament prophecy. **Mark** writes with urgency and action, portraying Jesus as the Servant who came to give His life. **Luke**, the physician, offers the most detailed and orderly account, emphasizing Jesus' compassion for the poor, the outcast, and the Gentile. **John** writes with theological depth, revealing Jesus as the divine Son of God through seven great signs and seven "I AM" declarations. Studying the Gospels requires comparing accounts, noting what each writer emphasizes and omits, and always asking: What does this reveal about the character and mission of Jesus?

### Studying the Epistles

The New Testament epistles (Romans through Jude) are **letters written to specific churches and individuals** addressing specific situations, yet they contain timeless theological truths and practical instruction for believers in every age. Studying an epistle requires understanding the occasion that prompted it, the relationship between the writer and the recipients, and the flow of the argument from beginning to end. Paul's letters, in particular, build tightly reasoned theological arguments — Romans is a systematic presentation of the gospel; Galatians is a passionate defense of justification by faith; Ephesians soars into the heights of God's eternal purpose for the church. Do not merely read epistles verse by verse; read them as complete letters, following the argument from greeting to benediction. Paul himself declared that "all scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness" (2 Timothy 3:16).

### Studying Revelation

The book of Revelation holds a unique promise: **"Blessed is he that readeth, and they that hear the words of this prophecy, and keep those things which are written therein: for the time is at hand"** (Revelation 1:3). This is the only book of the Bible that pronounces a specific blessing on its readers. As Seventh-day Adventists, we understand Revelation through the historicist lens, seeing its prophecies as spanning the entire Christian era from John's day to the second coming and beyond. Revelation is saturated with Old Testament imagery — the sanctuary, the feasts, Daniel's prophecies, the plagues of Egypt — and cannot be understood apart from its Old Testament roots. The student who has built a strong foundation in the Old Testament will find Revelation opening like a flower, revealing the triumphant conclusion of God's plan of salvation.`,
