        '"We have also a more sure word of prophecy; whereunto ye do well that ye take heed, as unto a light that shineth in a dark place, until the day dawn, and the day star arise in your hearts." — 2 Peter 1:19 (KJV)',
      teachingContent: `### Prophetic Bread for the Last Days

The showbread was called in Hebrew **lechem panim**, "the bread of the face" or "the bread of the Presence," because it stood continually before the face of God. Prophecy is God's face turned toward the future, revealing His purposes to His people before they unfold. **"Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets"** (Amos 3:7). Peter calls the prophetic word "a light that shineth in a dark place" (2 Peter 1:19), and in the spiritual darkness of earth's final hours, no light shines more brightly than the prophecies of Daniel and Revelation. For Seventh-day Adventists, prophetic study is not an elective — it is the bread that sustains the remnant through the time of trouble.

### Principles of Prophetic Interpretation

Interpreting Bible prophecy requires specific, biblically grounded principles. First, the **historicist method** reads the prophecies of Daniel and Revelation as spanning the course of history from the prophet's day to the end of time. This is the method used by the Protestant Reformers and by our Adventist pioneers, and it alone does justice to the progressive unfolding of prophetic time periods. Second, the **day-year principle** (Numbers 14:34; Ezekiel 4:6) provides the key to understanding time prophecies: the 2,300 days of Daniel 8:14, the 1,260 days of Revelation 12:6, and the 70 weeks of Daniel 9:24-27 represent years in prophetic fulfillment. Third, **symbols are interpreted by Scripture itself** — beasts represent kingdoms (Daniel 7:17, 23), waters represent peoples and nations (Revelation 17:15), a pure woman represents God's faithful church (Revelation 12:1), and a fallen woman represents apostasy (Revelation 17:1-5).

### The Prophecies of Daniel

The book of Daniel provides the **skeletal framework of prophetic history**. Nebuchadnezzar's image in Daniel 2 maps the rise and fall of empires from Babylon to the second coming. The four beasts of Daniel 7 add detail, introducing the little horn power and the heavenly judgment scene. Daniel 8 and 9 unfold the 2,300-day prophecy and the 70 weeks, pinpointing the time of the Messiah's appearance and the beginning of the investigative judgment in 1844. Daniel declared, "There is a God in heaven that revealeth secrets, and maketh known to the king Nebuchadnezzar what shall be in the latter days" (Daniel 2:28). The precise fulfillment of these prophecies across centuries of history is one of the most powerful evidences for the divine inspiration of Scripture.

### The Prophecies of Revelation

Revelation completes what Daniel began, carrying the prophetic narrative through the Christian era to the second coming, the millennium, and the new earth. The seven churches (Revelation 2-3) trace the spiritual history of the Christian church. The seven seals and seven trumpets unveil the political and religious upheavals of history. Revelation 12-14 presents the great controversy in its final phase, culminating in the three angels' messages — the special calling of the Seventh-day Adventist Church. **"The testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy"** (Revelation 19:10), and the student who faithfully studies these prophecies will stand with confidence as earth's final events unfold.`,
