      memoryVerse:
        '"And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also." — 2 Timothy 2:2 (KJV)',
      teachingContent: `### The Bread That Multiplies When Shared

The priests did not hoard the showbread for themselves. Though they ate it in the holy place, their ministry was fundamentally oriented toward serving the congregation of Israel (Leviticus 24:9). The bread they received was meant to sustain them **for service**. In the same way, everything we learn in Bible study is intended not only for personal nourishment but for the building up of others. The bread of God's Word **multiplies when it is shared**. This is the divine principle embedded in 2 Timothy 2:2: "And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also." Notice the four generations embedded in this single verse: Paul taught Timothy, Timothy was to teach faithful people, and those faithful people were to teach others. The gospel advances not through one teacher reaching millions but through each student becoming a teacher.

### The Call to Teach

The writer of Hebrews issues a sobering rebuke to those who remain perpetual students: **"For when for the time ye ought to be teachers, ye have need that one teach you again which be the first principles of the oracles of God; and are become such as have need of milk, and not of strong meat"** (Hebrews 5:12). Spiritual maturity is not measured by how much we know but by our ability to nourish others with what we have received. There comes a time when every believer should be able to open the Scriptures and guide another person into truth. This is not a calling reserved for pastors and professional evangelists. It is the calling of every disciple. Ezra modeled the complete cycle of spiritual leadership: **"For Ezra had prepared his heart to seek the law of the LORD, and to do it, and to teach in Israel statutes and judgments"** (Ezra 7:10). Notice the three-fold progression: seek (study), do (apply), and teach (share). You cannot teach what you have not studied, and you should not teach what you have not practiced.

### Preparing a Bible Study for Others

Preparing a Bible study for others follows a clear process. **First**, choose a topic or passage and study it thoroughly yourself using the methods you have learned in this course — observation, interpretation, application, word study, context, cross-referencing. **Second**, organize your material into a logical flow with a clear introduction, main points, and conclusion. **Third**, prepare questions that guide the learner into the text rather than simply lecturing them — the best Bible studies are dialogues, not monologues. **Fourth**, identify key memory verses and practical applications. **Fifth**, pray earnestly for the person or group you will be teaching, asking the Holy Spirit to prepare their hearts and guide your words. Nehemiah 8:8 describes the ideal: "They read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading." Clear reading, clear explanation, clear understanding — this is the goal.

### Teaching Deepens the Teacher

There is a well-established principle that we truly learn something only when we can explain it clearly to another person. **Preparing to teach forces you to think through the text more carefully**, anticipate questions, and articulate truth with precision. Questions from students will push you to study more deeply. Struggles you observe in others will reveal gaps in your own understanding. Teaching is not the graduation ceremony of Bible study; it is the continuation of it at a deeper level. Do not wait until you feel fully qualified — you will never feel fully qualified. Begin where you are: lead a small group discussion, study the Bible with a friend over lunch, teach a Sabbath School class, mentor a new believer, or guide your family through worship. The bread is meant to be shared, and the table is large enough for all who will come.`,
